WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.917
BRIAN SUK: Hey, Lorin.

00:00:00.917 --> 00:00:02.040
LORIN PRICE: Hey, Brian.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.470
BRIAN SUK: Did you know
that my sister's husband's

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.470
mother's uncle was married
to your great grandmother?

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.220
LORIN PRICE: Wait.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.260
How did you figure that out?

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.810
Does that mean we're related?

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.560
BRIAN SUK: Well, I
loaded our family trees

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.200
onto JanusGraph running
on Google Cloud Bigtable,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.060
ran some queries, and found out.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.560
No, I'm just kidding
with you, but I

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.352
can tell you some pretty
interesting things

00:00:22.352 --> 00:00:23.280
about the Greek gods.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.716
LORIN PRICE: Let's check it out.

00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:28.048
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.270
BRIAN SUK: JanusGraph
is an open source,

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.280
distributed graph database.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.330
Amongst its many
different features,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.480
one of the key
strengths of JanusGraph

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.830
is the idea of
pluggable storage.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.500
You can use various
storage back end

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.830
technology such as
Apache's Cassandra, HBase,

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.320
or in our case, we're going
to use Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.090
So let's go ahead and
create Bigtable cluster.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.263
We're going to call
this JanusGraph.

00:00:56.263 --> 00:00:57.680
And as you can see
here, there are

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.380
a number of different options
that you can configure.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.850
Let's set up a
production instance

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.720
to show what this looks like.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.570
We're going to choose
solid-state disk

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.720
since we are just testing this
and wants to run it quickly.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.480
We're also going to
deploy this in US central1

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.270
in the F zone, set it for
the minimum production

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.300
size of three nodes for a
cluster, and away we go.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.960
Now that our
cluster is deployed,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.680
we're going to use our Cloud
Shell to deploy JanusGraph

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.070
itself, which we're going to
deploy onto a Google Kubernetes

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.520
Engine cluster that we have also
created in advance for this.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.870
We will use Helm to set this up.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.110
While this is deploying, let's
take a look at the sample graph

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.420
that we're going to use
for the demonstration

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.670
and see what that looks like.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.710
Here is the sample graph
data that we will be

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.720
using for this demonstration.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.870
The sample data set is both
in the Google Cloud tutorial

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.200
and is also available on
the JanusGraph tutorial.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.990
The graph of the gods
outlines the relationships

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.510
between various
ancient Greek gods,

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.540
their pets, the monsters
they battled, and also

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.280
where they lived.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.550
Different entities that you
see here with attributes

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.400
are referred to as vertexes,
and the relationships

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.820
between those vertexes
are called edges.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.770
So for example, in the lower
left side of this graph,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.630
we can see that, at the start
of the vertex labeled Hercules,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.220
there is an edge
protruding from that

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.730
on the right that shows that
he has battled a monster named

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.200
Cerberus.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.170
What this enables us to do
is query the relationships

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.040
between different entities.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.950
When storing this
kind of information

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.440
in a regular
relational database,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.330
it typically requires
complex joints

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.480
to manage, especially so
when the level of joining

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.580
gets multiple layers deep.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.830
With graph traversal
languages, this

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.680
makes it far more intuitive
and easier to query

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.290
these different relationships.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.900
This also requires a new
language to query it,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.010
and in JanusGraph, we call
this language Gremlin.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.920
To start off with, let's
just run a simple command

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.620
to find the name of
Jupiter's brother.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.290
In a relational database,
you would perform a query

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.240
across two tables.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.280
Here, we see that this
command is starting

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.900
from a vertex, denoted g.V,
that has the name Jupiter,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.170
and we are looking for outward
relationships of type brother

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.080
and want the values
of those names.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.813
Neptune and Pluto
is what comes back,

00:03:23.813 --> 00:03:25.980
which matches with what's
on the graph on the right.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.860
Now let's look at
the edges themselves.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.530
Instead of just looking
up where Pluto lives,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.340
we now want to see why, which
is an attribute of an edge.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.770
So the query here
also starts at Pluto,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.600
but now we're looking
for an outward edge

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.440
denoting where he lives, and
we want to look at the reason.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.570
In a typical
relational database,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:55.020
this would always involve some
sort of gods table, a location

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.470
table, and most likely some
form of a cross-reference table

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.350
between the two.

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:03.500
You can start to see how graph
traversal makes these kinds

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.600
of operations far easier.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.276
Lastly, let's look at a
query with yet another join.

00:04:11.276 --> 00:04:14.420
Here, we want to see a
third level relationship

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.860
from Jupiter.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.050
In this case, we
want to see where

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.899
Jupiter's brother's dog lives.

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:24.380
If we see here, the query
simply just starts at Jupiter,

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.410
looks for the next level
relationship marked brother,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:31.970
then looks for his pet, and
finally returns where he lives.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.740
If we see the result
here, it's marked

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.020
as Tartarus, which
corresponds also

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.870
to the visual representation
of the graph on the right.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.410
So there you have a few quick
examples of graph traversal.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.680
And what's really cool about
this particular architecture

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.290
is that, because it is
backed by Google Cloud

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.840
Bigtable as a
storage mechanism, we

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.520
know that this
architecture will allow

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.640
this to scale as large as
the graph is going to grow.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.310
So now you've seen that by
using JanusGraph on Google Cloud

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.210
Platform, you can make some
pretty useful connections

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.980
in the data that you
wouldn't normally

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.130
be able to make in a
transactional SQL database.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.320
And also, if you want to run
this tutorial for yourself,

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.820
be sure to check
out the link below.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.445
LORIN PRICE: If you
enjoyed this video,

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:15.680
don't forget to like,
comment, and subscribe

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:17.930
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.190
Thanks for watching.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

